                 Modular Sculpt Testing on UP Extreme Edge

                             Christian Helmuth


Preparations
############

Test machine
============

* Reference hardware is UPX-EDGEI7-A10-1664-F01 with Samsung 970 EVO
  Plus NVMe (500 GB, M.2 2280)

* The used GPT partition scheme looks like follows.

  ! > sudo parted -s /dev/nvme0n1 "unit mib  print free"
  !
  ! Model: Samsung SSD 970 EVO Plus 500GB (nvme)
  ! Disk /dev/nvme0n1: 476940MiB
  ! Sector size (logical/physical): 512B/512B
  ! Partition Table: gpt
  ! Disk Flags: 
  ! 
  ! Number  Start      End        Size       File system     Name    Flags
  !         0,02MiB    1,00MiB    0,98MiB    Free Space
  !  1      1,00MiB    122MiB     121MiB     fat32           EFI     boot, esp
  !         122MiB     62622MiB   62500MiB   Free Space
  !  3      62622MiB   78247MiB   15625MiB   linux-swap(v1)  SWAP    swap
  !  4      78247MiB   140747MiB  62500MiB   ext2            GENODE
  !  5      140747MiB  390747MiB  250000MiB                  VM
  !  2      390747MiB  476939MiB  86192MiB   ext4            LINUX
  !         476939MiB  476940MiB  1,01MiB    Free Space

  * Partition 2 hosts a native Ubuntu 20.04.5 LTS installation.
  * Partition 4 hosts the Genode Sculpt "Default" file system
  * Partition 5 hosts the VM block device containing a virtual Ubuntu Server
    20.04.5 LTS installation.

* The device is booted from an iPXE prepared USB thumb drive for the Sculpt
  scenario that uses ethernet port LAN1.

* Debug log is captured from serial port COM1.

* The test machine is addressable via DNS as upx1 (alternatively replace
  upx1 by an IP address).


Native Ubuntu installation
==========================

Beside the standard desktop installation the following packages were
installed.

* virtualbox-qt 6.1.34-dfsg-3~ubuntu1.20.04.1

  * native VirtualBox for baseline benchmarks
  * virtual disk referencing /dev/nvme0n1p5 via disk.vmdk
    ! vboxmanage internalcommands createrawvmdk -filename disk.vmdk -rawdisk /dev/nvme0n1p5
  * VM configurations for 2 and 4 guest CPUs
    ! vboxmanage list vms
    !
    ! "Ubuntu Server 20.04 (cpu4)" {132b541f-ce59-4fb5-bb05-21941cf48a05}
    ! "Ubuntu Server 20.04 (cpu2)" {182ebcee-3b4b-4378-b70d-0bb8ba374780}

  * port-forwarding rule for easy access to guest openssh-server
    ! vboxmanage modifyvm "132b541f-ce59-4fb5-bb05-21941cf48a05" --natpf1 "guestssh,tcp,,2222,,22"
    ! vboxmanage modifyvm "182ebcee-3b4b-4378-b70d-0bb8ba374780" --natpf1 "guestssh,tcp,,2222,,22"
  * virtual Ubuntu Server 20.04.5 LTS installation

* openssh-server 8.2p1-4ubuntu0.5

  for remote terminal access to the host and the VM running in native
  VirtualBox

The standard user is "genode" and is permitted to SUDO any command.
The following command should successfully login from a remote machine.

! ssh -t genode@upx1


Virtual Ubuntu Server installation
==================================

Beside the standard server installation the following packages were
installed.

* moreutils 0.63-1

  For the "parallel" utility used in the tools described below.

* build-essential 12.8ubuntu1.1

  All tools required for building tools - used to prepare Tiobench.

* Tiobench c91485abf2b99ffa76e6ecf0a11bf2bc8b994699

  ! mkdir -p tiotest; cd tiotest
  ! git clone https://github.com/mkuoppal/tiobench tiobench.git; cd tiobench.git
  ! make

* stress-ng 0.11.07-1ubuntu2

  System stressor utility

The standard user is "genode" and is permitted to SUDO any command.
The following command should successfully login from a remote machine via
the native Ubuntu host (and later via Sculpt).

! ssh -p 2222 -t genode@upx1


Modular Sculpt configuration
============================

:sculpt/upx-pc.sculpt:

This file configures the used scenario and references the configuration
pieces described in the following.

The depot user could be changed by adapting the following lines. This step
is optional as the packages are not published but integrated directly into
the boot image.

! # configure depot user
! depot: <depot user>

:sculpt/deploy/upx:

The scenario deploys a Sculpt desktop (fonts_fs, themed_wm, system_clock-pc,
black_hole) with Default file system in RAM. Also, the NVMe partitions are
made available (nvme-0.part_block) with an additional file system on
partition 4 (nvme-0.4.fs) and the VM block device in partition 5. For
diagnostic purposes, the scenario also includes the trace_logger that logs
information about VirtualBox and the Kernel-Idle-Times.

On the desktop, the deploy configuration automatically runs the selected
VirtualBox variant (version 5/6; 1, 2, or 4 virtual CPUs) and required
components for shared file system (shared_fs), USB-passthrough config
(upx_usb_devices), and VirtualBox configuration chroot file system (upx_fs).

:sculpt/drivers/upx:

The drivers subsystem is quite similar to the original Sculpt drivers/pc except
the disabled, non-working (and quite busy looping) PS/2 driver.

:sculpt/launcher/upx_fs:

Sandbox for VirtualBox config file system in RAM (/vm/upx).

:sculpt/launcher/upx_usb_devices:

Empty USB device config ROM.

:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox5_cpu1:
:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox5_cpu2:
:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox5_cpu4:
:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox6_cpu1:
:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox6_cpu2:
:sculpt/launcher/upx_vbox6_cpu4:

Launchers for variants of VirtualBox 5/6 with 1, 2, or 4 virtual CPUs. Note
the routes to upx_fs and nvme-0.part_block.

:sculpt/nic_router/upx:

NIC router configuration using the ethernet driver by default. It also
includes a port-forwarding rule similar to native Ubuntu for external port
2222 to VirtualBox port 22.

:sculpt/ram_fs/upx:

The RAM file system configuration imports the depot.tar (depot packages) and
upx.tar (VirtualBox configuration) ROM from the boot image.


Build and run
#############

:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox5_cpu1:
:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox5_cpu2:
:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox5_cpu4:
:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox6_cpu1:
:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox6_cpu2:
:./recipes/pkg/upx_vbox6_cpu4:
:./recipes/raw/upx:

The scenario uses these packages for variants of VirtualBox 5/6 on
1, 2, and 4 CPUs. This is necessary as the number of CPUs must be configured
in the .vbox/.vbox6 file.

:./run/upx.run:

The following steps are required to build and run the scenario.

* add repos/upx to REPOSITORIES

* setup appropriate RUN_OPT, for example

  ! RUN_OPT += --depot-auto-update
  ! RUN_OPT += --amt-tool wsman
  ! RUN_OPT += --include power_on/amt
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --power-on-amt-host upx1-amt
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --power-on-amt-password 'PASSWORD'
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --power-on-amt-timeout 15
  ! RUN_OPT += --include log/serial
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --log-serial-cmd 'picocom -b 115200 /dev/ttyUSB0'
  ! RUN_OPT += --include image/uefi
  ! RUN_OPT += --include load/ipxe
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --load-ipxe-base-dir /tftpboot/
  ! RUN_OPT +=   --load-ipxe-boot-dir /upx1
  ! # enable the following to start lighttpd on demand - port must match ipxe config
  ! #RUN_OPT +=   --load-ipxe-lighttpd
  ! #RUN_OPT +=   --load-ipxe-lighttpd-port 2209

* run the scenario in the build directory

  ! make run/upx KERNEL=nova BOARD=pc CCACHE=yes DEPOT_USER=chelmuth


Tools and benchmarking
######################

The tools can be used to benchmark the VM, and must, therefore, be
copied onto the virtual disk (e.g., by using scp).

:tool/upx_dd:

* runs one instance of _dd_ per CPU to write a total of 16 GiB to disk

:tool/upx_tiotest:

* runs Tiobench's tiotest [1] and stores results amended by VMM information
  (VirtualBox version, number of CPUs)

* requires tiobench.git clone in _home/genode/tiotest/tiobench.git

* requires SUDO rights

* usage example: start 1000 threads to sequentially write 16 MiB each (16
  GiB total) with a buffer size of 1 MiB

  ! upx_tiotest -k 1 -k 2 -k 3 -L -F -b 1048576 -t 1000 -f 16"

After the scenario has started on Genode or VirtualBox on native Ubuntu, the
VM can be benchmarked and controlled from remote like follows.

Login for interactive use

! ssh -p 2222 -t genode@upx1

Power off the VM (e.g., before rebooting)

! ssh -p 2222 -t genode@upx1 sudo poweroff

Run dd based test

! ssh -p 2222 -t genode@upx1 tiotest/upx_dd

Run Tiobench benchmark

! ssh -p 2222 -t genode@upx1 tiotest/upx_tiotest -k 1 -k 2 -k 3 -L -F -b 1048576 -t 1000 -f 16

